spi: dw: prefill enhanced TX PIO before enabling SER clocks

An enhanced transmit frame ends when the TX FIFO drains. The existing
PIO path starts the frame with the controller SER channel active and then
writes instruction, address and payload entries. At quad clocks the FIFO
can therefore begin draining before software has finished filling a short
frame, producing a truncated or corrupted transfer even though the whole
frame fits in the FIFO.

The DMA path already avoids this race by holding native SER inactive while
the control words and a payload prefix are queued. Apply the same rule to
non-DMA enhanced writes: temporarily gate native SER, preload the complete
control+payload frame, then restore SER so wire clocks begin only after the
FIFO is ready. This internal clock gate also applies when physical CS is
managed by a Linux SPI GPIO.

This is controller framing, not peripheral policy. Ordinary single-line
transfers and enhanced RX retain their existing behavior. If native SER is
already inactive because an outer path is intentionally gating it, leave
ownership with that caller and only perform the prefill.

diff --git a/drivers/spi/spi-dw-core.c b/drivers/spi/spi-dw-core.c
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-dw-core.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-dw-core.c
@@ -1 +1,11 @@
-	if (dws->enh.ctrl_len) {
+	u32 saved_ser = 0;
+	bool gate_enh_tx = false;
+
+	if (dws->enh.ctrl_len) {
+		gate_enh_tx = dws->tx_len && !dws->dma_mapped;
+		if (gate_enh_tx) {
+			saved_ser = dw_readl(dws, DW_SPI_SER);
+			if (saved_ser)
+				dw_writel(dws, DW_SPI_SER, 0);
+		}
+
@@ -10,6 +20,10 @@
 
 		if (dws->tx_len && !dws->dma_mapped)
 			dw_writer(dws);
+
+		/* MMIO FIFO writes complete before native CS starts the frame. */
+		if (gate_enh_tx && saved_ser)
+			dw_writel(dws, DW_SPI_SER, saved_ser);
 	} else if (!dws->tx_len) {
 		dw_writel(dws, DW_SPI_DR, 0);
 	}
